        'en' => [
            'title' => 'Nanjing Yunjin: Court Brocade on the Large-Pattern Loom',
            'excerpt' => 'Yunjin, centered on Nanjing, is woven on a two-person large-pattern loom with gold and zhuanghua color changes; inscribed by UNESCO in 2009 as a key court-textile tradition.',
            'content' => '<p>Yunjin (cloud brocade) is the Nanjing-centered tradition of patterned silk weaving that, among China’s “Four Famous Brocades,” is known for dense florals, metallic threads, and court use. The name evokes cloud-like luminosity: woven surfaces shift with light as motifs seem to layer. In 2009, the craftsmanship of Nanjing Yunjin brocade was inscribed on UNESCO’s Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity, making it a key reference for Ming–Qing court textiles and Jiangnan silk systems.</p>
<h2>Historical arc</h2>
<p>Yunjin’s technical roots reach back through official weaving around Nanjing from the Six Dynasties onward; the craft matured in the Yuan–Ming period and peaked under the Qing Jiangning weaving administration. Court decrees, ritual colors, and ranked motifs kept Yunjin reserved for dragon robes, rank badges, and ceremonial hangings. After official workshops dissolved in the modern era, local ateliers and later research institutes rebuilt pattern books, looms, and process records so large-pattern wooden looms could continue.</p>
<p>Unlike Shu brocade’s warp-faced lineage or Song brocade’s mounting culture, Yunjin emphasizes weft patterning and metallic density. Reading Yunjin means separating technical routes inside “Jiangnan brocade,” not labeling every glittering silk as Yunjin.</p>
<h2>Craft and structure</h2>
<p>The signature tool is the large-pattern wooden loom worked by two people: one draws the pattern from the upper storey while the other throws the shuttle below. Pattern cords govern warp lifts. Ground warps are typically mulberry silk; gold and silver threads—and historically peacock-feather yarn—supply ornamental wefts. Complete production may involve more than a hundred steps, from yarn selection and dyeing through warping, loom dressing, pattern drawing, weaving, and finishing.</p>
<p>Zhuanghua is Yunjin’s most distinctive category: discontinuous weft brocading lets neighboring motifs take different colors on the same course. Other grades such as kujin and kusatin emphasize ground texture and metallic sheen. Zhuanghua should be understood as a high-difficulty branch within Yunjin, not a separate “fifth famous brocade.”</p>

<h2>Motifs, color, and use</h2>
<p>Traditional motifs include dragons, phoenixes, clouds, bats, the Eight Treasures, and scrolling florals. Saturated yellows, reds, blues, and greens are often outlined or filled with gold. Court garments and hangings encode rank; festive civilian dress borrows cloud-and-gold imagery at lower material density. Appraisal should check institutional motifs, metallic luster, and the reverse: true discontinuous wefts leave local returns rather than printed or embroidered cover.</p>
<figure class="hanfu-article-figure" data-slot-id="evidence" data-visual-role="evidence"><img src="/pub/media/blog/textile-heritage/yunjin/inline-1.jpg" alt="Qing Qianlong yellow-ground dragon robe in Yunjin, Grassi Museum Leipzig" width="1200" height="800" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Qing Qianlong yellow-ground dragon robe, a court-grade Yunjin specimen. <span class="hanfu-article-figure__provenance"><a class="hanfu-article-figure__source" href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Drachenrobe-Qianlong.JPG" rel="noopener noreferrer">Grassi Museum</a> · <a class="hanfu-article-figure__license" href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/" rel="noopener noreferrer">CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h2>Identification and common misreadings</h2>
<p>Not every gold-threaded figured silk is Yunjin. Verify loom tradition, discontinuous weft practice, and Nanjing workshop lineage. Treating zhuanghua as an independent fifth brocade confuses a technique category with a regional brand. Machine jacquard can mimic the face design yet rarely matches hand brocading’s color changes and metallic hand; the reverse and joins remain decisive.</p>
<h2>Living heritage and Hanfu use</h2>
<p>Today Yunjin appears in museums, ICH workshops, and limited bespoke formalwear. In Hanfu practice it suits ranked round-collar robes, cloud collars, belts, or selective borders—not all-over stage glitter. Matching motif rank to weave density respects historical logic; when budgets are tight, reserve authentic zhuanghua for collars, cuffs, or badges and use related satin grounds for the body.</p>
<p>From a materials perspective, gold thread and peacock feather are not mere luxury tags: metal changes reflectance, while peacock barbules create structural color shifts as the viewer moves. That is why Yunjin displays reward raking light and motion that still photos miss. “More than a hundred steps” is process reality—tension, dye fastness, pattern proofing, and drawing rhythm interlock, and faults show at scale joints and cloud breaks.</p>
<p>On the Jiangnan map, Yunjin complements Songjin and Shujin: court metallic density, mounting geometry, and warp-faced roundels respectively. Choosing by scene rather than “most expensive brocade wins” follows historical division of labor.</p>
<h2>At a glance</h2>
<ul>
<li>Center: Nanjing; UNESCO ICH inscription in 2009.</li>
<li>Loom: two-person large-pattern wooden loom; often 100+ process steps.</li>
<li>Materials: silk ground with gold/silver thread and historically peacock feather.</li>
<li>Signature category: zhuanghua discontinuous weft brocading; court uses include dragon robes.</li>
<li>Caution: zhuanghua is not a separate “fifth brocade.”</li>
</ul>
