        'en' => [
            'title' => 'Chengdu Shu Brocade: Warp Tradition and Pearl-Roundel Tang Style',
            'excerpt' => 'Shu brocade, centered on Chengdu, pairs warp-faced figuring with Silk Road pearl motifs; Laoguanshan looms and Tang paired-bird silks anchor its deep history.',
            'content' => '<p>Shu brocade (Shujin) is the ancient silk brocade centered on Chengdu—“Brocade City”—and one of China’s Four Famous Brocades. Its core tradition is warp-faced patterning: colored warps carry the design while wefts chiefly bind the structure. Longstanding sericulture and official weaving in Shu made the cloth both a domestic prestige textile and a vehicle for Silk Road motifs such as pearl roundels and paired birds.</p>
<h2>Historical arc</h2>
<p>“Brocade City” binds industry to urban identity. From the Han onward, official and private weaving coexisted on the Chengdu plain. Museum holdings show Tang pearl-roundel paired birds and animals as localized versions of Sogdian–Persian design language. Jacquard-related models from the Laoguanshan Han tombs supply material evidence for early pattern control, so complex figuring in Shu is not only a textual claim.</p>
<p>After Song–Yuan shifts toward Jiangnan weaving, Shu brocade persisted as a regional brand. Modern revival projects rebuilt pattern sets while contemporary “Shujin” discourse also includes later weft and satin variants—yet warp-faced tradition remains the best starting point.</p>
<h2>Craft and structure</h2>
<p>In warp brocade, colored warps rise by pattern draft; wefts stabilize the ground. Color bands and units often run with the warp, suiting roundels and mirrored birds. Laoguanshan loom research offers a baseline for comparing later pattern harnesses.</p>
<p>Judge “Shu character” by warp figuring, Chengdu pattern lineage, and layered Silk Road motifs—not glitter alone. Against Yunjin discontinuous wefts and Songjin mounting geometries, Shu narrates ancient warp brocade and cross-cultural motif migration.</p>

<h2>Motifs, color, and use</h2>
<p>Classics include pearl roundels, paired birds/beasts, tree-florals, and cloud fills in strong Tang reds, blues, and yellows. Uses ranged from official dress and furnishings to trade goods; today museums, ICH garments, and cultural products dominate. In Hanfu, pearl-and-bird vocabularies suit Tang round-collar robes and shawls when clearly labeled as motif citation rather than tomb replicas.</p>
<figure class="hanfu-article-figure" data-slot-id="evidence" data-visual-role="evidence"><img src="/pub/media/blog/textile-heritage/shujin/inline-1.jpg" alt="Tang Shu brocade with pearl roundel and paired birds, Chengdu Museum" width="1200" height="800" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Tang Shu brocade with pearl roundels and paired birds, a warp-faced classic. <span class="hanfu-article-figure__provenance"><a class="hanfu-article-figure__source" href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shu_brocade,_Chengdu_Museum.png" rel="noopener noreferrer">Chengdu Museum</a> · <a class="hanfu-article-figure__license" href="https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Public Domain Mark 1.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h2>Identification and common misreadings</h2>
<p>Not every Chengdu figured silk equals warp-faced Shu tradition. Pearl roundels are often locally woven fusions, not “imported Persian cloth.” Do not apply Nanjing zhuanghua criteria. Prints lose warp color depth and reverse evidence.</p>
<h2>Living heritage and Hanfu use</h2>
<p>Chengdu Museum and the China National Silk Museum make Tang Shu brocade publicly legible. For Hanfu, match roundel scale to the body; if translating motifs into print or damask, disclose the difference from true brocade.</p>
<p>Pearl-roundel history warns that motif migration is not outsourced weaving: Tang Shu paired birds were often redesigned locally. Hanfu citations should state period, region, and medium layers. Laoguanshan looms move “early figuring” from text into mechanical comparison; even with modern equipment, warp figuring and pattern control remain the factual core.</p>
<h2>At a glance</h2>
<ul>
<li>Center: Chengdu; one of the Four Famous Brocades; warp-faced tradition.</li>
<li>Key evidence: Laoguanshan Han looms; Tang pearl-roundel bird silks.</li>
<li>Structure: colored warp figuring; symmetrical roundels common.</li>
<li>Differentiate from Yunjin zhuanghua and Songjin mounting logic.</li>
<li>Hanfu tip: Tang round-collar forms align well with pearl motifs when provenance is clear.</li>
</ul>
