        'en' => [
            'title' => 'Suzhou Embroidery: Pattern Made by the Needle',
            'excerpt' => 'Suxiu is one of the Four Famous Embroideries, not a brocade—valued for even lustrous stitches and double-sided work; contrasting it with Yunjin and Songjin clarifies embroidery versus weaving.',
            'content' => '<p>Suzhou embroidery (Suxiu) is the Suzhou-centered needlework system counted among China’s Four Famous Embroideries, alongside Yue, Xiang, and Shu embroidery. It is not a brocade: images are added with needle and thread on a ground cloth, not generated by interlacing warps and wefts. Suxiu is prized for stitches that are flat, even, fine, dense, harmonious, lustrous, smooth, and uniform, and for forms such as double-sided embroidery. Placing it in a textile-heritage series clarifies weaving versus embroidery so Hanfu fabric talk does not conflate them.</p>
<h2>Historical arc</h2>
<p>Suzhou’s silk weaving and domestic needlework grew together: brocades and satins supplied grounds while embroidery translated rank badges, auspicious images, and painterly taste. In Ming–Qing times Suxiu served dress, hangings, fans, and independent pictorial panels for longevity themes. Modern institutes systematized stitch vocabularies and double-sided display pieces, moving Suxiu from household craft into teachable ICH practice.</p>
<p>Near Songjin and not far from Yunjin geographies, Suxiu remains post-loom decoration, not loom patterning. A garment may combine Songjin borders with Suxiu sprays—but their craft identities must stay distinct.</p>
<h2>Craft and structure</h2>
<p>Core practice includes splitting silk, satin and long-and-short fills, seed knots, and related stitches. Finer splits enable denser, flatter surfaces; color nesting seeks harmony and even sheen. Double-sided work manages both faces for screens and exhibition pieces. Grounds may be satin, tabby, or gauze, with tension tuned to avoid puckering.</p>
<p>Structurally, embroidery sits on an existing weave and does not rewrite the ground’s warp–weft identity—unlike Songjin, Shujin, or Yunjin figuring. Judge split quality, stitch direction, and rhythm, not merely “Suzhou-looking” subjects.</p>

<h2>Motifs, color, and use</h2>
<p>Subjects span birds-and-flowers, figures, landscapes, antiquities, and longevity scenes such as Immortals Celebrating Longevity. Colors prefer graded transitions over hard blocks. Uses divide into dress embroidery and pictorial panels. In Hanfu, Suxiu fits collars, cloud collars, mamian accents, or badge-scale motifs; avoid overweight panels on airy gauzes.</p>
<figure class="hanfu-article-figure" data-slot-id="evidence" data-visual-role="evidence"><img src="/pub/media/blog/textile-heritage/suxiu/inline-1.jpg" alt="Qing Suzhou embroidery “Immortals Celebrating Longevity,” Shanghai Museum" width="1200" height="800" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Qing Suzhou embroidery “Immortals Celebrating Longevity,” noted for even, lustrous needlework. <span class="hanfu-article-figure__provenance"><a class="hanfu-article-figure__source" href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:苏绣灵仙祝寿图.jpg" rel="noopener noreferrer">Shanghai Museum</a> · <a class="hanfu-article-figure__license" href="https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Public Domain Mark 1.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h2>Identification and common misreadings</h2>
<p>Do not list Suxiu among the Four Famous Brocades—it is embroidery. Machine embroidery and printed faux stitch can mimic outlines but rarely match hand-split sheen or true double-sided consistency. Subject alone does not prove Suzhou authorship; stitch systems and workshop lineage do. Do not grade embroidery by Yunjin gold-brocade criteria.</p>
<h2>Living heritage and Hanfu use</h2>
<p>Contemporary Suxiu thrives in ICH studios and bespoke work. Embroidery is accent; weave is structure. Fine silk-ground handwork on edges beats all-over machine fill. Double-sided elements suit fans, pouches, and reversible ornaments more than full robe faces.</p>
<p>Suxiu and Songjin share a city yet split labor: weave for structure, embroidery for narrative accents. Qing pictorial embroidery reaches painterly shading without erasing dress embroidery’s ritual roles. Learn basic fills before double-sided display techniques. In markets, magnify splits, turns, and reverse quality to separate handwork from print or machine fill—and keep saying “embroidery, not brocade.”</p>
<h2>At a glance</h2>
<ul>
<li>Center: Suzhou; one of the Four Famous Embroideries; needlework, not brocade.</li>
<li>Aesthetics: flat, even, fine, dense, harmonious, lustrous, smooth, uniform; includes double-sided work.</li>
<li>Uses: dress embroidery and pictorial panels; longevity and bird-flower themes common.</li>
<li>Identify by split silk and stitch systems, not subject stereotypes alone.</li>
<li>Hanfu: local accents; never conflate with famous brocades.</li>
</ul>
