        'en' => [
            'title' => 'Zhuanghua: Discontinuous Color within Yunjin',
            'excerpt' => 'Zhuanghua is Yunjin’s signature discontinuous-weft category that lets neighboring motifs change color; it is not a fifth famous brocade, but the key to Yunjin’s labor and splendor.',
            'content' => '<p>Zhuanghua is the signature technical category within Nanjing Yunjin: on satin or related grounds, discontinuous weft brocading (digging and coiling colored wefts into motif areas) allows neighboring flowers to take different colors on the same course. It is not a fifth famous brocade beside Shu, Song, and Yunjin; it is the high-difficulty branch that explains Yunjin’s splendor and labor cost.</p>
<h2>Historical arc</h2>
<p>Zhuanghua intensified with Ming–Qing court needs for finely graded color on dragons, flames, and cloud collars—needs continuous throw changes could not meet. Large-pattern looms and pattern cords around Jiangning supported this warp-continuous, weft-discontinuous practice. Early Ming zhuanghua satins such as The Met’s lotus, Eight Treasures, and phoenix panel (2001.471) show metal and colored silk working together, aligning with court-use records.</p>
<p>Modern talk sometimes isolates “zhuanghua” as if it were a peer brand. The correct nesting is Yunjin (regional system) ⊃ zhuanghua (technique/category), with kujin/kusatin as sibling faces.</p>
<h2>Craft and structure</h2>
<p>Colored wefts—including gold—enter only where motifs need them, saving precious yarn and enabling local color change. Weavers continually switch small bobbins along pattern boundaries; two-person large looms keep harness and shuttle in sync. The face is polychrome; the reverse often shows returns and piles of discontinuous wefts—key evidence of hand zhuanghua.</p>
<p>Zhuanghua may sit on satin or gauze grounds (“zhuanghua satin,” “zhuanghua gauze”). Those names describe ground + technique, not independence from Yunjin. Versus patterned luo gauze: luo is gauze weave with leno crossings and often subtle damask voids; zhuanghua gauze is decorative discontinuous weft on a gauze ground—related in conversation, not identical.</p>

<h2>Motifs, color, and use</h2>
<p>Scrolling lotuses, Eight Treasures, phoenixes, dragons, and clouds dominate; neighbor-motif recoloring looks more fragmented than Songjin throws and more lively than ordinary jacquard. Metal threads outline veins and auspicious details. Use is high-status dress and furnishings; in Hanfu, reserve zhuanghua for badges, borders, or ritual zones rather than everyday full coverage.</p>
<figure class="hanfu-article-figure" data-slot-id="evidence" data-visual-role="evidence"><img src="/pub/media/blog/textile-heritage/zhuanghua/inline-1.jpg" alt="Early Ming zhuanghua satin with lotuses, Eight Treasures and phoenixes, Met 2001.471" width="1200" height="800" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Early Ming zhuanghua satin (Met 2001.471), showing discontinuous multicolored wefts. <span class="hanfu-article-figure__provenance"><a class="hanfu-article-figure__source" href="https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/62477" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Met 2001.471</a> · <a class="hanfu-article-figure__license" href="https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/" rel="noopener noreferrer">CC0 1.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h2>Identification and common misreadings</h2>
<p>Zhuanghua is not a fifth brocade. Machine polychrome seldom matches hand discontinuous returns. “Zhuanghua gauze” ≠ patterned luo. Do not judge by face glitter alone—check reverse and collection data.</p>
<h2>Living heritage and Hanfu use</h2>
<p>Nanjing ICH workshops and museums remain the reference. Treat zhuanghua as Yunjin’s premium option: authentic accents on kusatin or figured grounds. Label copy as “Yunjin zhuanghua” rather than a free-floating “zhuanghua brocade.”</p>
<p>Zhuanghua’s cost is information density: each motif may recolor, so weavers manage many small bobbins against upper-loom draws, plus metal-thread stiffness and breakage. The Met’s early Ming panel offers a public benchmark for orderly fragmentation of lotus, treasures, and phoenixes. Keep the sentence “zhuanghua is a signature Yunjin weaving category” to block “fifth brocade” myths and point readers back to the large-pattern system.</p>
<h2>At a glance</h2>
<ul>
<li>Position: signature Yunjin category/technique, not a fifth brocade.</li>
<li>Core craft: discontinuous weft brocading with per-motif color.</li>
<li>Loom context: two-person large-pattern loom; zhuanghua satin common.</li>
<li>Versus luo: related gauze talk possible; weave definitions differ.</li>
<li>Hanfu: ritual accents; always state Yunjin affiliation.</li>
</ul>
